> If the probability of an event happening in some single attempt is p,
> after which it cannot happen again, then the probability it will happen
> within n attempts is 1 - (1 - p)^n.  (The chance of it _not_ happening
> in any attempt is (1 - p).  The chance of it not happening twice in a
> row is (1 - p)^2, not 2 * (1 - p).  Etc.)
> 
> So the chance you would wrest a charge from an empty wand within 99 zap
> attempts is about 0.56.  The chance a cursed wand would explode within
> 99 zap attempts is about 0.63.

Well, those numbers are only right if the two events are independent,
but in fact they are not (once one of them happens, the other one can't).

Writing out the true probability seems difficult, so I wrote a little
program to estimate it.  Zapping 10 million simulated wands until they
either exploded or gave a wish (counting exploding as happening first),
I got a wish 45.0% of the time.  If I limit that to 99 zaps, I got a
wish 37.6% of the time (only did 200,000 trials on this one).


